# 2. The ontological starting point: manifestation cannot be external to itself

Abhinavagupta’s most secure argument concerns *prakāśa*, manifestation or luminous appearing.

Anything that can be:

* asserted;
* denied;
* perceived;
* inferred;
* remembered;
* imagined;
* doubted;

must occur within manifestation.

If something were wholly outside every possible manifestation, then no evidence for it could appear.

Thus:

[
\boxed{
\operatorname{Established}(x)
\rightarrow
\operatorname{Manifest}(x).
}
]

Even the statement:

> Manifestation does not exist

appears as a manifest cognition.

This does not prove that every object is merely a private mental image.

It proves the transcendental priority of manifestation:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{no account of reality can eliminate the fact of}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{appearing without presupposing it in the elimination.}
}
]

Abhinavagupta’s *Tantrāloka* is a 37-chapter synthesis of Trika ritual, theology and philosophy, while the Pratyabhijñā works provide much of the explicit argumentative defence of this nondual Śaiva framework. ([Internet Archive][1])

## Inexternalist translation

Physical science can describe:

[
\operatorname{Ext}(P)
=====================

\text{relations, transitions, quantities and causal structure}.
]

But even a complete extrinsic description does not logically contain:

[
\operatorname{Int}(P)
=====================

\text{the fact that anything is manifest}.
]

The first inexternalist axiom is therefore:

[
\boxed{
\operatorname{Ext}(P)
\text{ cannot replace }
\operatorname{Int}(P).
}
]

That does not prove a cosmic mind.

It establishes only that manifestation is not derived merely by redescribing nonmanifest relations.

