# 3. Vimarśa: why manifestation cannot be inert

A passive light could illuminate objects without being aware of itself.

Abhinavagupta therefore joins *prakāśa* to *vimarśa*.

Vimarśa means more than reflective introspection. It includes:

* reflexive awareness;
* determinate apprehension;
* self-articulation;
* recognition;
* expressive power;
* the capacity to present something as something.

The contrast is:

[
\operatorname{Prakāśa}(x)
=========================

x\text{ is present},
]

[
\operatorname{Vimarśa}(x,\alpha)
================================

x\text{ is present as }\alpha.
]

A red field is not merely undifferentiated manifestation.

It is:

[
\text{manifest-as-red}.
]

A remembered friend is:

[
\text{manifest-as-previously-encountered}.
]

An action is:

[
\text{manifest-as-possible-and-mine-to-perform}.
]

Recent scholarship on *vimarśa* emphasizes that it functions in Utpaladeva and Abhinavagupta as a metaphysical process of reflexive articulation rather than merely as an occasional mental act. ([Academia][2])

## Inexternalist translation

Manifestness alone does not explain a differentiated world.

We require:

[
M
+
Q_i
+
K_i
+
V_i
\rightarrow
D_i,
]

where:

* (M): manifest capacity;
* (Q_i): participant-specific distinctions and invariances;
* (K_i): coupling and organization;
* (V_i): salience and valence;
* (D_i): one determinate phenomenal domain.

Vimarśa corresponds most closely to the whole active organization by which manifestation becomes determinate.

It cannot be reduced to a metacognitive self-model.

A computational self-model is one possible finite mechanism within vimarśa-like articulation. Abhinavagupta’s claim is ontologically stronger:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{manifestation is inherently capable of determinate}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{self-presentation.}
}
]

