# 5. The thirty-six tattvas as a possibility-space decomposition

The tattvas can be grouped into three large domains:

## Pure tattvas

[
1\text{–}5
]

The articulation of universal manifestation before finite separation.

## Pure–impure tattvas

[
6\text{–}12
]

The formation of restricted perspective through Māyā, the five kañcukas and puruṣa.

## Impure tattvas

[
13\text{–}36
]

The organization of cognition, sensation, action and material embodiment.

The standard order begins with Śiva, Śakti, Sadāśiva, Īśvara and Śuddhavidyā, followed by Māyā, the five limiting powers, puruṣa, prakṛti, the cognitive faculties, sensory and motor capacities, subtle qualities and gross elements. ([Hindu Blog][3])

The tattvas do not describe chronological events before the Big Bang.

They are best interpreted as logical dependencies:

[
T_{n+1}
]

expresses a more restricted or determinate condition than:

[
T_n.
]

The order is therefore:

[
\text{ontological differentiation},
]

not necessarily:

[
\text{temporal creation sequence}.
]

