# PART ONE — THE PURE TATTVAS

# 6. Tattva 1: Śiva

## Traditional function

Śiva tattva represents the pole of pure subjectivity:

[
\boxed{
\text{“I.”}
}
]

Objectivity is not absent in an absolute sense, but remains wholly implicit.

The accent falls on pure manifest presence.

In later explanatory language:

[
\text{aham}
\gg
\text{idam}.
]

## Inexternalist reconstruction

Śiva tattva corresponds to the irreducible fact:

[
\boxed{
\exists M.
}
]

Something is manifest.

At this level, we should not yet infer:

* a human-like subject;
* a universal personality;
* an autobiographical self;
* a deliberating cosmic agent.

The minimal content is:

[
\boxed{
\text{intrinsic presence without determinate objectification}.
}
]

This is the strongest defensible core of Śiva tattva.

## Frontier assessment

The inference:

[
\text{manifestness}
\rightarrow
\text{one universal agent}
]

is not yet valid.

Śiva tattva therefore has two readings:

### Minimal reading

[
M=\text{manifest capacity}.
]

### Maximal Śaiva reading

[
M=\text{one omnipotent universal subject}.
]

Inexternalism accepts the minimal reading and leaves the maximal reading unproved.

