# 7. Tattva 2: Śakti

## Traditional function

Śakti is not a second substance beside Śiva.

It is Śiva’s capacity to manifest, know, desire and act.

If Śiva is manifestation as pure presence, Śakti is manifestation as active power:

[
\boxed{
M\rightarrow\operatorname{PotentialArticulations}(M).
}
]

The distinction is conceptual, not ontological separation.

## Inexternalist reconstruction

Śakti is the possibility structure of process.

Let:

[
\Omega(P)
]

be the space of transformations available to process (P).

Then:

[
\operatorname{Ext}(P)
]

includes its structured possibilities, not merely its present state.

A thing is partly defined by:

* what it can become;
* what it can affect;
* which counterfactuals it supports;
* which transformations preserve it.

Śakti can therefore be reconstructed as:

[
\boxed{
\Omega_M
========

\text{the structured power of manifestation to assume}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{determinate forms without becoming external to itself}.
}
]

## Relation to complexity science

Modern complexity theory studies:

* possibility spaces;
* attractor landscapes;
* reachable states;
* transition rules;
* symmetry breaking.

Levin’s morphospace and Wolfram’s computational possibility structures are contemporary attempts to formalize large spaces of potential form and transformation.

But these models do not show that possibility space is itself conscious.

Śakti’s exact modern status remains:

[
\boxed{
\text{structured possibility is necessary for articulation;}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{its identification with conscious power remains metaphysical.}
}
]

