# 8. Tattva 3: Sadāśiva

## Traditional function

Sadāśiva is usually expressed as:

[
\boxed{
\text{“I am this.”}
}
]

with predominance of “I.”

Objectivity emerges, but remains enveloped by subjectivity:

[
\text{aham}

>

\text{idam}.
]

Scholarship on Abhinavagupta’s Pratyabhijñā commentaries describes Sadāśiva as a level at which objectivity remains implicit or covered by predominant I-ness. ([Brill][4])

## Inexternalist reconstruction

This is the first polarity:

[
\boxed{
\text{manifest centre}
\rightarrow
\text{nascent field}.
}
]

The subject is not yet a finite organism.

The logical form is:

[
M
\rightarrow
(M,\delta M),
]

where (\delta M) is an emerging differentiation still experienced as internal to one manifest whole.

This resembles the initial formation of figure against an undifferentiated background.

## Complexity correspondence

Sadāśiva can be compared to early symmetry breaking.

A symmetric state acquires an incipient distinction, but the distinction is not yet independent.

Formally:

[
G
\rightarrow
H,
]

where a higher symmetry (G) is restricted to a subgroup (H), creating new distinguishable states.

This is only an analogy.

Abhinavagupta is not describing physical spontaneous symmetry breaking.

The shared logical structure is:

[
\boxed{
\text{difference emerges without severing dependence on unity}.
}
]

