# 9. Tattva 4: Īśvara

## Traditional function

Īśvara is expressed as:

[
\boxed{
\text{“This am I.”}
}
]

Here objectivity becomes dominant:

[
\text{idam}

>

\text{aham}.
]

The world becomes clearly articulated, while remaining recognized as inseparable from manifestation.

## Inexternalist reconstruction

Sadāśiva emphasized:

[
\text{field-as-owned-by-manifest-centre}.
]

Īśvara emphasizes:

[
\text{determinate-field-as-manifest}.
]

This corresponds to full phenomenal differentiation before strict subject–object alienation.

The world appears as:

* structured;
* plural;
* determinate;
* internally related;

but not yet as metaphysically independent of manifestation.

## Cognitive correspondence

A generative model does not merely receive unstructured data.

It constructs:

* objects;
* relations;
* causes;
* affordances;
* temporal patterns.

Īśvara can therefore be interpreted as the logical moment of **world disclosure**:

[
\boxed{
M
+
Q
\rightarrow
W.
}
]

But the Śaiva claim is stronger than predictive processing.

Predictive processing describes the construction of an organism-relative model.

Īśvara tattva describes universal manifestation’s ability to display an objective world.

