# 10. Tattva 5: Śuddhavidyā

## Traditional function

Śuddhavidyā establishes balance:

[
\boxed{
\text{“I am this, and this is I.”}
}
]

Neither subject nor object dominates.

[
\text{aham}
\approx
\text{idam}.
]

Difference is present without alienation.

## Inexternalist reconstruction

This is the pure form of inexternality:

[
\boxed{
\operatorname{Int}(P)
\equiv
\operatorname{Ext}(P).
}
]

Not identity of description:

[
\operatorname{Int}(P)
=====================

\operatorname{Ext}(P),
]

but numerical identity of referent:

[
\operatorname{Int}(P)
\text{ and }
\operatorname{Ext}(P)
]

are two aspects of one occurrence.

Śuddhavidyā is therefore the deepest tattvic analogue of process inexternalism.

It says:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{the manifest knower and the manifested world are}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{distinguishable poles within one event, not two}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{independently existing substances.}
}
]

## Frontier significance

This does not entail that every local subject shares one field.

The inexternalist correction is:

[
\operatorname{Ground}(D_i)
==========================

\operatorname{Ground}(D_j)
]

does not imply:

[
D_i=D_j.
]

Pure nonalienation at the ontological level must be distinguished from phenomenal co-presence at the subject level.

That distinction is not sufficiently developed in Abhinavagupta.

