# PART TWO — MĀYĀ AND THE CONSTRUCTION OF FINITUDE

# 11. Tattva 6: Māyā

## Traditional function

Māyā introduces the appearance of separation.

It does not create a second substance outside Śiva.

It restricts the way manifestation apprehends its own articulations:

[
\boxed{
\text{relational difference}
\rightarrow
\text{apparently independent entities}.
}
]

Māyā is therefore not simply false perception.

It is the condition under which:

* unity is hidden;
* difference is absolutized;
* finite subjects and objects appear mutually external.

## Inexternalist reconstruction

Māyā is reification.

Given an observer-specific quotient:

[
Q_i:X\rightarrow X/{\sim_i},
]

the observer treats the resulting objects as independently given rather than as outputs of:

* organismic distinctions;
* relational constraints;
* histories;
* action possibilities.

The error is:

[
[x]_{Q_i}
\rightarrow
\operatorname{SelfGrounding}(x).
]

That is:

[
\boxed{
\text{a relationally constituted appearance is mistaken}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{for an independently existing substance.}
}
]

## Scientific correspondence

Modern perception science supports the idea that experienced objects depend on:

* sensory systems;
* learned invariances;
* action capacities;
* generative models;
* bodily organization.

But this does not mean there is no mind-independent reality.

The disciplined inexternalist position is:

[
\boxed{
\text{experienced objects are relational actualizations,}
}
]

not:

[
\boxed{
\text{arbitrary inventions of consciousness}.
}
]

