# 12. The five kañcukas as the operators of subject formation

The five kañcukas restrict powers traditionally attributed to universal consciousness.

They transform:

[
\text{unrestricted manifestation}
]

into:

[
\text{a finite action-centred perspective}.
]

The five are commonly listed as:

* kalā: limited agency;
* vidyā: limited knowledge;
* rāga: incompleteness or desire;
* kāla: temporal limitation;
* niyati: causal and situational restriction. ([Hinduism Stack Exchange][5])

They can be reconstructed as the minimal constraint-set required for a local participant.

Let:

[
L_i
===

(A_i,K_i,V_i,T_i,C_i).
]

Then:

[
D_i
===

\Lambda_\Phi(M,L_i,\Sigma_i).
]

The local subject is not created by adding a soul-object.

It is created by **restriction of access and possibility**.

This is one of the most profound insights in the tattva system.

