# 13. Tattva 7: Kalā — limited agency

Universal agency becomes:

[
A_\infty
\rightarrow
A_i.
]

The finite system can perform only a restricted set of transformations.

[
\mathcal U_i
\subset
\mathcal U_\infty.
]

## Complexity-science translation

An agent is partly defined by its action space:

[
\mathcal A_i
============

{a_1,\ldots,a_n}.
]

Its world is shaped by what it can do.

A handle is visible as graspable only to a system capable of grasping.

Kalā therefore contributes to perspective by defining:

[
\boxed{
\text{the locally reachable region of possibility space}.
}
]

Levin’s concept of a cognitive light cone—the scale of goals and states a system can represent and pursue—is structurally close to this restricted action horizon.

## What Abhinavagupta adds

Limited agency is not merely a defect.

It is a condition of concrete action.

An agent with literally unrestricted possibilities would have no determinate task, trajectory or location.

Thus contraction is also individuation:

[
\boxed{
\text{to act concretely is to exclude most possible actions}.
}
]

