# 15. Tattva 9: Rāga — incompleteness, value and desire

Rāga is often translated as attachment or desire.

Its deeper role is that a finite participant experiences itself as lacking:

[
\boxed{
\text{current state}
\neq
\text{preferred state}.
}
]

This introduces normative distance:

[
\Delta V_i
==========

## V_i(x_{\text{preferred}})

V_i(x_{\text{current}}).
]

## Complexity-science translation

A self-maintaining system occupies a viable region:

[
\mathcal V_i
\subset X.
]

Some states promote continuation.

Others threaten dissolution.

The system therefore possesses endogenous norms:

[
x\in\mathcal V_i
\quad\text{or}\quad
x\notin\mathcal V_i.
]

Semantic information is information whose destruction reduces viability.

Rāga corresponds structurally to the formation of:

* stakes;
* salience;
* attraction;
* aversion;
* goal direction;
* organism-relative meaning.

## The consciousness frontier

This is our most important remaining bridge:

[
\boxed{
\text{objective viability}
\stackrel{?}{=}
\text{felt valence}.
}
]

Abhinavagupta treats desire as a contracted form of primordial fullness:

[
\text{fullness}
\rightarrow
\text{experienced lack}.
]

Modern biology explains why a system regulates itself.

It does not yet explain why dysregulation hurts.

Rāga therefore marks the frontier between:

[
\text{normativity}
]

and:

[
\text{feeling}.
]

