## Difference from Sāṅkhya

Sāṅkhya treats puruṣas as distinct witnessing principles.

Trika absorbs puruṣa into a larger process of contraction: the individual is a finite phase of transindividual awareness rather than a fully independent eternal witness. Abhinavagupta’s Pratyabhijñā commentaries explicitly treat the finite soul as a contracted phase of transindividual consciousness. ([Brill][4])

## Inexternalist correction

Puruṣa should not be identified immediately with:

[
\text{one universal subject pretending to be local}.
]

The safer conclusion is:

[
\boxed{
\text{a local subject is a real processual restriction of}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{a more general capacity for manifestation}.
}
]

Whether the general capacity is itself one subject remains open.

