* causal location.

The lower tattvas explain how that perspective becomes:

* cognitively organized;
* sensorimotorly coupled;
* qualitatively structured;
* materially stabilized.

The malas explain how useful finite distinctions become reified.

The upāyas explain how a multiscale process may be reorganized.

Recognition explains how the observer-model ceases to exempt and isolate itself.

## What remains unexplained

The tattvas do not supply a demonstrated law for:

[
\boxed{
\text{why one causal organization constitutes one}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{phenomenally co-present field}.
}
]

Abhinavagupta answers:

[
\text{because all organization is already self-manifest consciousness}.
]

That avoids emergence from absolute nonmanifestness.

But it does not yet explain why:

[
D_i\neq D_j.
]

The kañcukas describe restriction.

They do not formally derive phenomenal exclusion.

