# 49. The deepest decipherment

The thirty-six tattvas describe one movement:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{the conversion of unrestricted possibility into}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{a locally actionable world.}
}
]

To become a finite subject, manifestation must acquire:

1. a distinction between self and field;
2. limited access;
3. limited agency;
4. intrinsic stakes;
5. temporal depth;
6. causal location;
7. cognitive selection;
8. ownership attribution;
9. sensory discriminations;
10. action possibilities;
11. stable material embodiment.

This is not merely a fall from perfection.

It is the formation of a participant.

The bondage occurs when the participant concludes:

[
\boxed{
\text{these restrictions are an independently existing}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{self rather than the conditions of this perspective}.
}
]

Recognition is the discovery:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{I am not a spectator trapped inside a body confronting}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{an intrinsically external world.}
}
]

Rather:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{I am the intrinsic manifest presence of this embodied}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{world-involving process.}
}
]

The strongest Śaiva extension is:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{and this local process is an articulation of one}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{universal self-manifest power.}
}
]

The first claim is the core of process inexternalism.

The second remains a metaphysical hypothesis.

